%I A401144 #14 Oct 21 2026 20:06:57
%S A401144 4,4,4,4,18,6,17,5,16,7,15,5,6,14,10,5,13,9,9,5,12,8,8,19,65,11,7,7,
%T A401144 18,18,64,10,6,6,72,14,17,63,9,10,5,7,71,13,16,62,8,16,9,70,6,70,12,7,
%U A401144 17,61,7,15,8,23,69,5,69,11,11,6,16,60,6,14,7,14,22,68,10,68,10,14,10,10
%N A401144 Number of steps starting from n until the first repeated value under the map k -> (floor(6*k/5)+1)/5^v_5(floor(6*k/5)+1), where v_5(m) is the 5-adic valuation of m; or -1 if no value ever repeats.
%C A401144 a(n) counts applications of the reduced map T starting at n, up to and including the step that first returns to a previously visited value. Let G(k) = floor(6*k/5)+1 and T(k) = G(k)/5^v_5(G(k)). All factors of 5 are removed in a single iteration.
%C A401144 Equivalently, writing r = k mod 5, T(k) = (6*k+5-r)/5^v_5(6*k+5-r). The residue-dependent constants 5-r are 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 for r = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, respectively.
%C A401144 The four-cycle 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 1 is directly verified. A start on this cycle has a(n) = 4.
%C A401144 If the orbit has preperiod mu and eventual cycle length lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%C A401144 The author's accompanying paper reports an exhaustive finite first-descent computation for every starting integer 1 <= k <= 10^11, establishing entry into the displayed four-cycle throughout that finite range. This is not a proof of global convergence.
%C A401144 The paper distinguishes reduced (macro) steps from elementary steps and measures first descent; this sequence counts reduced iterations until the first repeated value, which is a different statistic.
%H A401144 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="/A401144/b401144.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401144 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22856438">The Reduced (floor(6k/5)+1)/5 Collatz-Type Domain with One Observed Attractor up to 10^11: Complete 5-Adic Reduction, Residue Algebra, Critical Multiplicative Statistics, Cycle Constraints, and Exhaustive First-Descent Verification</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A401144 Let G(k) = floor(6*k/5)+1, T(k) = G(k)/5^v_5(G(k)).
%F A401144 a(n) = min { j >= 1 : T^j(n) = T^i(n) for some 0 <= i < j }, if such j exists; otherwise a(n) = -1.
%F A401144 Equivalently, for an eventually periodic orbit with preperiod mu and period lambda, a(n) = mu + lambda.
%e A401144 For n = 1, the orbit begins 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 1. The first repeated value occurs after 4 iterations, so a(1) = 4.
%e A401144 For n = 2, the orbit begins 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 1 -> 2. The first repeated value occurs after 4 iterations, so a(2) = 4.
%e A401144 For n = 4, the orbit begins 4 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4. The first repeated value occurs after 4 iterations, so a(4) = 4.
%e A401144 For n = 5, the orbit begins 5 -> 7 -> 9 -> 11 -> 14 -> 17 -> 21 -> 26 -> 32 -> 39 -> 47 -> 57 -> 69 -> 83 -> 4 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4. The first repeated value occurs after 18 iterations, so a(5) = 18.
%e A401144 For n = 6, the orbit begins 6 -> 8 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 1 -> 2. The first repeated value occurs after 6 iterations, so a(6) = 6.
%t A401144 A401144[n_] := Length[NestWhileList[#/5^IntegerExponent[#, 5] & [Quotient[6*#, 5] + 1] &, n, UnsameQ, All]] - 1;
%t A401144 Array[A401144, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 21 2026 *)
%o A401144 (Python)
%o A401144 def T(k):
%o A401144     y = 6*k//5 + 1
%o A401144     while y % 5 == 0:
%o A401144         y //= 5
%o A401144     return y
%o A401144 def a(n):
%o A401144     k, seen = n, set()
%o A401144     while k not in seen:
%o A401144         seen.add(k)
%o A401144         k = T(k)
%o A401144     return len(seen)
%o A401144 # This program assumes eventual repetition; it does not decide divergence.
%Y A401144 Cf. A399120, A399761, A400174, A401143 for other first-repeat sequences under reduced residue-dependent maps.
%K A401144 nonn,new
%O A401144 1,1
%A A401144 _Farhad Banazadeh_, Oct 16 2026
